@node Performance Breakdown
@section Performance Breakdown: Where Every Second Goes
@cindex performance breakdown
@cindex prefill dominance

@subsection The codebud warm run (67.4 s) decomposed

@example
 67.4 s total wall-clock
 │
 ├─ 0.25 s  load check (Ollama confirms model is warm)
 │
 ├─ 57.19 s  PREFILL  ←── 84.9% of total time
 │            1,046 tokens processed at 18.3 tok/s
 │            The model reads the full planning prompt.
 │            Nothing can be shown to the user during this phase.
 │
 ├─  7.49 s  GENERATION
 │            62 tokens generated at 8.3 tok/s
 │            The JSON plan is produced here, token by token.
 │            The progress indicator shows the token counter.
 │
 └─  1.41 s  CODEBUD OVERHEAD
              Python startup, prompt build, HTTP, JSON parse,
              plan validation, step execution, memory write.
@end example

@noindent
@strong{Prefill is 84.9% of total time.}  Generation and all Codebud
overhead together account for less than 14% of the total.

@subsection The same breakdown for the short prompt (1.65 s)

@example
 1.65 s total (Ollama reported)
 │
 ├─ 0.15 s  load check
 ├─ 0.44 s  PREFILL   ←── 26.7% of total time (36 tokens at 81.8 tok/s)
 └─ 0.96 s  GENERATION  ←── 58.2% of total time (10 tokens at 10.4 tok/s)
@end example

@noindent
With a short prompt, generation dominates --- the model spends more time
producing output than reading input.  With the planning prompt, the ratio
inverts completely.

@subsection Why prefill is slower per token with a longer prompt

Prefill speed was @strong{81.8 tok/s} for 36 tokens but only
@strong{18.3 tok/s} for 1,046 tokens --- a 4.5× slowdown per token.

This is a direct consequence of the @dfn{self-attention} mechanism.
In each transformer layer, every token must attend to every other token.
The computation cost scales with the @emph{square} of sequence length:

@example
36-token prompt:   36 × 36  =  1,296 attention pairs per layer
1046-token prompt: 1046 × 1046 = 1,094,116 attention pairs per layer
                   = 844× more work

Ratio of prefill times: 57.19 / 0.44 = 130×
Expected ratio from token count alone: 1046 / 36 = 29×

The actual ratio is higher than linear (130× vs 29×) because attention
is super-linear, but lower than quadratic (844×) because the CPU can
parallelise some of the computation across its 8 threads.
@end example


@node Assessment
@section Assessment
@cindex assessment
@cindex optimisation directions
@cindex prompt length

This is the assessment as written in May 2026.  It led to the work
described in @ref{October 2026 Results}, which changed several of these
conclusions; it is kept here as the record of that reasoning.

@subsection What the numbers tell us (May 2026)

@enumerate

@item
@strong{The planning prompt is the dominant cost --- not model size, not
generation length, not tool overhead.}

57 out of 67 seconds (85%) are spent on prefill.  If you want to make
Codebud faster, reducing the planning prompt length is the highest-leverage
action.  Switching to a faster CPU or a faster disk has minimal effect
on a warm-model run.

@item
@strong{Cold and warm runs cost the same.}

Cold and warm runs were within 1 second of each other (68.3 s vs 67.4 s);
see @ref{Test 4 Codebud End-to-End} for why that is probably because the
model was still loaded, not because of the pre-warm.

@item
@strong{Codebud's Python overhead is 1.4 s --- negligible.}

The framework (AgentCore, planner, executor, safety checks, memory write)
adds under 2% to the total time.  This is not the bottleneck.

@item
@strong{The @command{llm} CLI baseline is not a fair competitor.}

@command{llm} sends 36 tokens; Codebud sends 1,046 tokens.  They are
doing different things.  A meaningful comparison would be: 9.6 s
(@command{llm}) vs 1.65 s (Ollama Test 2) are both ``raw model speed''
measurements.  The 65.6 s (Codebud) is the cost of structured JSON planning
on a CPU.

@end enumerate

@subsection Optimisation directions, ranked by expected impact

@multitable @columnfractions .35 .20 .45
@headitem Action @tab Expected saving @tab How
@item Reduce planning prompt to ~400 tokens @tab ~25--30 s @tab Remove examples, shorten rules, use terser tool descriptions
@item Switch to 7B model on GPU @tab ~55 s @tab GPU prefill is 10--50× faster; 57 s → 1--6 s
@item Use a dedicated small model for planning @tab ~40 s @tab A 1.5B model prefills ~2× faster
@item Cache the plan for repeated identical requests @tab ~65 s @tab Hash (user message + context); skip Ollama if cached
@item Reduce context window (@code{num_ctx}) @tab ~5--15 s @tab Smaller KV cache = faster prefill at cost of shorter memory
@end multitable

@noindent
@strong{None of the following will make a meaningful difference:}
reducing Python startup time, optimising JSON parsing, reducing HTTP
overhead, tuning generation parameters.  The bottleneck is purely prefill.

@subsection What ``beginner-friendly'' means here

In May 2026, a simple request took about a minute.  (Today the first
request of a session takes about 30 s and later ones about 8 s; see
@ref{October 2026 Results}.)  If you wonder where that time goes:
@strong{the model is reading.}

Before it can write a single character of output, it must read and
process every character of the 1,046-token planning prompt --- the system
instructions, the tool list, the examples, your question.  On a CPU
without a dedicated GPU, this reading takes 57 seconds.  The answer
itself (62 tokens of JSON) then takes about 7 more seconds.

It is not a bug.  It is not a hang.  The progress spinner showing
``Thinking... 57s (prefill)'' is the model doing real work.


@node October 2026 Results
@section October 2026 Results
@cindex prompt caching
@cindex KV prefix cache
@cindex num_thread

Measured on 2026-10-24 on the same laptop with Ollama 0.40.6 and the same
model.  Three things changed the picture since May.

@subsection 1. Ollama reuses the cached prompt prefix

Codebud's planning prompt starts with the same instructions, tools and
examples every time; only the conversation history and the request at the
end change.  Ollama keeps the key/value data of the last prompts it
processed and only prefills what differs:

@multitable @columnfractions .40 .20 .20 .20
@headitem Request @tab Prompt tokens @tab Prefill @tab Wall time
@item Chat turn 1, nothing cached @tab 1,046 @tab 29.9 s @tab 32.4 s
@item Chat turn 2 @tab 1,092 @tab 2.6 s @tab 4.9 s
@item Chat turn 3 @tab 1,123 @tab 2.0 s @tab 4.4 s
@item @code{codebud plan}, new process, prefix cached @tab --- @tab --- @tab ~8 s
@end multitable

The cache survived unrelated requests in between (the heartbeat's
@samp{hi}, a different prompt), so after the first request of a session the
planning prompt's length matters much less than the May assessment
assumed.  Ollama 0.40.6 is also about twice as fast on the cold prefill as
0.22 (29.9 s against 57.2 s for the same 1,046 tokens).

@subsection 2. A fixed context window

Ollama 0.40 loads models with a 4,096-token context unless told otherwise,
and silently drops the @emph{start} of a longer prompt --- Codebud's
instructions.  Codebud now asks for 8,192 tokens on every request
(@env{CODEBUD_NUM_CTX}) and trims the oldest conversation turns when a
prompt would not fit.  The value must be the same on every request: a
different @code{num_ctx} makes Ollama reload the model and lose the cache,
which is why lowering it per request is not a useful optimisation.

@subsection 3. CPU threads

Cold prefill of the planning prompt by @code{num_thread} (one run each):

@multitable @columnfractions .25 .25 .25 .25
@headitem @code{num_thread} @tab Prefill @tab Prefill tok/s @tab Generation tok/s
@item 2 @tab 46.1 s @tab 22.7 @tab 7.8
@item 3 @tab 37.3 s @tab 28.0 @tab 9.2
@item 4 @tab 31.1 s @tab 33.6 @tab 10.3
@item 8 @tab 31.7 s @tab 33.0 @tab 10.5
@item default @tab 35.7 s @tab 29.3 @tab 10.3
@end multitable

Four threads (the number of physical cores) is the sweet spot; Ollama's
default is within about 10%.  Since prefill is now mostly cached, this only
affects the first request of a session, and Codebud leaves the default.

@subsection What this means now

@itemize @bullet
@item The first request of a session costs about 30 s on this laptop,
almost all of it reading the prompt; later requests take about 8 s.
@item Shortening the planning prompt now mainly speeds up that first
request.
@item Python overhead remains negligible (about 1.4 s, of which imports
take about 22 ms).
@end itemize


@node The Measurement Program
@section The Measurement Program
@cindex measurement program
@cindex benchmark script

@file{scripts/benchmark.py} reproduces the measurements: it sends a short
prompt and the full Codebud planning prompt to Ollama and prints Ollama's
own timings.  It sends the same @code{num_ctx} as Codebud, bypasses HTTP
proxies for a local Ollama, and imports Codebud from @file{src/}.  Run it
from the repository root in the development environment
(@pxref{Build System}), with @env{OLLAMA_MODEL} set:

@example
python scripts/benchmark.py                 @r{# short and planning prompts}
python scripts/benchmark.py --warm-first    @r{# load the model before timing}
python scripts/benchmark.py --prompt "..."  @r{# time your own prompt}
python scripts/benchmark.py --json          @r{# machine-readable results}
@end example


@node Project Search Evaluation
@section Project Search Evaluation
@cindex project search, evaluation
@cindex recall
@cindex MRR

@file{scripts/rag_eval.py} measures how well project search
(@pxref{The index Subcommand}) finds the right files.
@file{scripts/rag_eval.json} holds 32 questions about Codebud's own
source, each with the files that answer it: 23 @dfn{concept} questions in
plain words (``how is a file replaced atomically so a crash never leaves
it half written?'') and 9 @dfn{identifier} questions naming a symbol
(``where is compact_history defined?'').  For each ranking variant the
script reports:

@table @asis
@item recall@@k
the share of questions with a correct file among the top @var{k} results;
@item MRR
the mean reciprocal rank: 1 when a correct file comes first, 1/2 when
second, @dots{}, 0 when none is found;
@item ms/q
the mean search time.
@end table

Run it from the repository root in the development environment, with
Ollama running and the index built:

@example
codebud index
python scripts/rag_eval.py                  @r{# all variants, k = 5}
python scripts/rag_eval.py -k 3 --quiet     @r{# top 3, no list of misses}
python scripts/rag_eval.py --json           @r{# machine-readable results}
@end example

The repository's @file{.codebudignore} keeps the question file out of the
index: it holds the questions verbatim, so indexing it would make keyword
search look far better than it is.

@subheading Results, October 2026

@code{nomic-embed-text}, this repository (95 files, about 1,170 chunks):

@multitable @columnfractions .34 .13 .10 .13 .13 .12
@headitem Variant @tab recall@@5 @tab MRR @tab concept @tab ident. @tab ms/q
@item vector only @tab 0.97 @tab 0.75 @tab 0.96 @tab 1.00 @tab 113
@item keyword (BM25) only @tab 0.91 @tab 0.64 @tab 0.91 @tab 0.89 @tab 4
@item keyword + symbol names @tab 0.94 @tab 0.71 @tab 0.91 @tab 1.00 @tab 15
@item hybrid (all three) @tab 0.94 @tab 0.76 @tab 0.91 @tab 1.00 @tab 143
@item hybrid, tests x 0.5 (default) @tab 0.97 @tab 0.83 @tab 0.96 @tab 1.00 @tab 146
@item hybrid, tests x 0.25 @tab 0.97 @tab 0.83 @tab 0.96 @tab 1.00 @tab 146
@end multitable

Embeddings carry the concept questions; keyword and symbol matching are
nearly free and carry the identifier ones; fusing them gives the best
ranking (MRR).  Its one extra miss against vectors alone was a concept
question whose top results were test files: tests mention every name they
test, so they crowd out the code that defines it.  Codebud therefore
halves the score of chunks from test files (@file{tests/}, @file{test_*},
@file{*_test.py}) unless the question is about tests: recall@@5 rose to
0.97 and MRR to 0.83; at @var{k} = 3, from 0.91 to 0.94 and from 0.76 to
0.82.  A quarter instead of a half changed nothing, so the gentler factor
was kept.  The questions are few and were written by the developers, so
small differences mean little.

@subheading Embedding models

The same eval with three embedding models, each building a fresh index
of this repository (1,184 chunks) on the same CPU:

@multitable @columnfractions .26 .12 .12 .14 .12 .12 .12
@headitem Model @tab size @tab index @tab vector R@@5 @tab hybrid R@@5 / MRR @tab hybrid R@@3 / MRR @tab ms/q
@item @code{all-minilm} @tab 45 MB @tab 66 s @tab 0.88 @tab 0.97 / 0.83 @tab 0.97 / 0.83 @tab 25
@item @code{nomic-embed-text} @tab 274 MB @tab 522 s @tab 0.97 @tab 0.97 / 0.83 @tab 0.94 / 0.82 @tab 148
@item @code{qwen3-embedding:0.6b} @tab 639 MB @tab about 55 min (projected) @tab --- @tab --- @tab --- @tab ---
@end multitable

On their own, @code{nomic-embed-text}'s vectors are clearly better
(recall@@5 0.97 against 0.88), but with keyword and symbol matching added
the two give the same results, and @code{all-minilm} indexes eight times
faster and searches six times faster.  It became the default.  Its limits:
it reads only about the first 256 tokens of a chunk, and it was trained on
English; projects with long prose or other languages may do better with
@code{nomic-embed-text}.  @code{qwen3-embedding:0.6b} took about 2.8@tie{}s
per chunk, so its run was stopped: an hour per first index is not
practical on a CPU, and the hybrid results above leave it at most one
question to gain.


@node Planning with Project Context
@section Planning with Project Context
@cindex model choice, edits
@cindex plan evaluation

Finding the right file is half the job; the planner still has to use it.
@file{scripts/plan_eval.py} plans six requests about Codebud's own source
--- five edits (``change the RRF constant used for fusing rankings from 60
to 50'') and one lookup --- with project context from the index, and
counts a plan as correct when its steps name the file that answers the
request and give every tool the arguments it requires.  Nothing is
executed.

@example
codebud index
python scripts/plan_eval.py qwen2.5-coder:3b-instruct-q4_K_M \
                            qwen2.5-coder:7b-instruct-q4_K_M
@end example

@subheading Results, October 2026

Same CPU, @code{all-minilm} index:

@multitable @columnfractions .40 .18 .22 .20
@headitem Model @tab correct @tab valid arguments @tab per plan
@item @code{qwen2.5-coder:3b-instruct-q4_K_M} @tab 0 of 6 @tab 1 of 6 @tab 117 s
@item @code{qwen2.5-coder:7b-instruct-q4_K_M} @tab 5 of 6 @tab 6 of 6 @tab 192 s
@end multitable

The 7B model read and then patched the right file for all five edits; for
the lookup (``how are chat sessions locked against a second resume?'') it
read @file{cli/main.py} and the usage chapter, which describe the lock,
rather than @file{sessions.py}, which implements it.  The 3B model ignored
the context and its tools' arguments, for example:

@example
@{"tool": "file_read",  "args": @{"file_path": "path/to/your/file"@}@}
@{"tool": "file_write", "args": @{"file_path": "path/to/your/file",
                               "line_number": "line_number_found", @dots{}@}@}
@end example

@noindent
So the 3B model suits questions and lookups, and the 7B model edits
(@pxref{Choosing a Model}).

